-- title:
--   Valuative cover of the blown-up supersingular chart by its ends
-- statement:
--   **Arithmetic data.** Let $q \ge 5$ and $\ell \ge 3$ be primes with $\ell \ne q$, and let $M' \ne 0$ be a natural number with $q \nmid M'$ and $\ell \nmid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic $0$ and $\xi \in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ which (`hK`) is the $L$-subfield generated by the image under coefficientwise base change $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q}) \to \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ of the $q$-expansion function field of level $\Gamma_H$, where the level is $N_0 = (q\ell)^2 M'$ and $H = \mathrm{levelH}(q\ell, M')$ is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   Let $A$ be a henselian discrete valuation ring which is an $L$-subalgebra with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, with $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ in the image of $A$ (`hξA`), and let $K$ be an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$ be non-zero whose image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of the modular $j$-function (`hj`). Let $\varpi$ generate $\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hϖ`), and let $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C = \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K\,j$ for the subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, with distinguished element $\mathrm{jChartFin} = j$, and $\mathfrak{X} = \mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\,A\,K\,j$ for the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the two chart algebras along the middle chart, with its structure morphism $\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   **The supersingular point.** Let $y \subset C$ be a maximal ideal (`hy`) containing $\varpi$ (`hϖy`) which is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no non-zero $q$-torsion point.
--
--   **The rigidity block `hArig`.** This hypothesis asserts, for every point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$ at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under $\mathrm{toBase}$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ whose ideal is supersingular in the above sense, the existence of a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0, X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$, and an isomorphism $e$ of the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/(\,C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f\,u\,)$, subject to a list of riders (summarised here): $e$ carries germs of constants $a \in A$ to the classes of $C(\sigma a)$; every $\tau$ which is a level automorphism of $K$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserves $C$, and for $\gamma$ moreover in $\Gamma(\ell)$ acts trivially modulo $y'$; for such $\tau$ acting trivially modulo $y'$ there are $\theta \in \operatorname{Aut}(S)$, $c \in W$ and $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ with $\theta$ matching $\tau$ on germs, fixing constants, with $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of $(X_0,X_1)$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, with $c \equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$ and $c \not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$; primes of $S$ over $(\sigma\varpi)$ not containing both $X_0, X_1$ and carrying distinct tangent directions $(a_1 : b_1)$, $(a_2 : b_2)$ modulo $q$ have distinct contractions to the stalk; for such a prime with tangent direction $(1:0)$, a germ of $a \in C$ lies in its contraction exactly when every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_A$; and, for the element $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,L\,(q\ell)$ of $K$ (the $j$-expansion with $q$ replaced by $q^{q\ell}$) and a suitable $a_0 \in A$, the germ of $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,L\,(q\ell) - a_0$ corresponds under $e$ to the class of a series $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, $e_0 \ge 1$, whose degree-$e_0$ form takes unit values at all $(a,b) \in W^2$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$.
--
--   **The chosen point and the pinned chart.** Fixed are a point $z$ of $\mathfrak{X}$ with germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hϖz`, `hz`), a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with $\mathrm{ιFin}(y') = z$ (`hy'`), supersingular (`hss'`), and with $y'$ equal to $y$ as an ideal (`hy'y`). Fixed further are a complete discrete valuation ring $W_1$, a homomorphism $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`), power series $f_1, u_1, v_1 \in W_1[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u_1, v_1$ units (`hu₁`, `hv₁`) and $f_1 \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\,C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1\,)$. The hypothesis `hW₁` requires of this particular witness exactly the list of riders displayed in the conclusion of `hArig`, instantiated at $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1$ (constants, stabilisation of $C$ by level automorphisms, triviality modulo $y'$ on $\Gamma(\ell)$, the linearisation $(\theta, c, M)$ with its congruences and the branch conditions at $\Gamma(\ell)$ and $\Gamma(q)$, separation of distinct tangent directions, the coefficient criterion for the direction $(1:0)$, and the clause for $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,L\,(q\ell)$ with $e_0$ and $h$).
--
--   **Centre, chart and exceptional valuation.** Let $\mathrm{germY} : C \to \mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X},z}$ be the canonical map of the chart algebra into the stalk at $z$ through the open image of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, and $\mathrm{toC}$ the map of the stalk into its adic completion. The ideal $J \subseteq C$ is pinned by `hJ` as the infimum, over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, of the pullback along $\tau$ of the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. The $A$-subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is pinned by `hB` as the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K : x\varpi_t \in J\}$, that is $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$. Let $\mathcal{W}$ be a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`), subject to:
--
--   `hR1`: $C \subseteq B$, and every element of $K$ is a quotient of two elements of $B$ with non-zero denominator.
--
--   `hR2`: $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation, and $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$.
--
--   `hR3` (five clauses): an element of $L$ lies in $\mathcal{W}$ exactly when it comes from $A$; $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{W}}$ is generated by $\varpi$; $\mathcal{W}$ is a discrete valuation ring; an element of $C$ lies in $y$ exactly when it lies in $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{W}}$; and $\mathcal{W}$ is the localisation of $B$ at this centre, i.e. $f \in \mathcal{W}$ iff $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{W}}$.
--
--   `hEQ` (five clauses): first, for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho : B \to \mathrm{CoordRing}\,q\,k$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring with kernel the set of elements of $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{W}}$, compatible with $A \to k$, and equivariant in the sense that for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $\mathcal{W}$ there is $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in the kernel of the character $(g, c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$ such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathrm{hAction}\,q\,k$ at that element composed with $\rho$, and with $c \ne 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$; second, level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B$; third, every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is the image of the centre of $\mathcal{W}$ under some level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; fourth, an element of $B$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_{\mathcal{W}}$ after all such translations is divisible by $\varpi$; fifth, a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on $C$ preserves $\mathcal{W}$.
--
--   Here a level automorphism of $K$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ means that $\tau$ satisfies `IsLevelAutAt` for the data $(L, q\ell, \xi, q\ell, (q\ell)^2M', \mathrm{levelH}(q\ell,M'))$: for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f, g$ of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M', H)$ with integral $q$-expansions and $g \ne 0$, and all $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the quotient of those of $f$ and $g$, the image of $\tau x$ under any $\iota$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$ satisfies $\iota(\tau x) \cdot (g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)\gamma^{-1})^\wedge = (f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)\gamma^{-1})^\wedge$ on $q$-expansions.
--
--   **The ends.** A finite set $\mathrm{ends}$ of subrings of $K$ is pinned by `hends`: a subring $O$ belongs to it exactly when there are $a \in J$ with non-zero image in $K$ and a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a = C[J/a]$, the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K : xa \in J\}$, such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ (that is $f \in O$ iff $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), every element of $y$ which lies in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$, and $B \not\subseteq O$. Finally, `hperm` asserts that for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $\mathcal{W}$, each $O \in \mathrm{ends}$ has some $O' \in \mathrm{ends}$ with $f \in O \iff \tau f \in O'$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses, for every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ such that an element of $L$ lies in $V$ exactly when it comes from $A$, such that $C \subseteq V$, and such that every element of $y$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_V$, at least one of the following holds: either $B \subseteq V$; or there is an end $O \in \mathrm{ends}$ such that every $f \in O$ lies in $V$ and, if $f$ is not a unit of $O$, lies in $\mathfrak{m}_V$, i.e. $V$ dominates $O$.
--
--   This is the valuative-criterion step for the blow-up of the two-chart integral model at the weighted centre $J$ over a supersingular point $y$: it says that the $\varpi_t$-chart $B$ together with the finitely many ends forms a valuative cover of the blown-up curve above $y$, so that no valuation of $K$ centred on $y$ escapes both. It is the third conjunct of the assembly [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_commonChart_and_stabilizer_and_valuativeCover_ends_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_commonChart_and_stabilizer_and_valuativeCover_ends_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked), whose hypotheses it shares.
